{
  "analysis": "Both responses provide comprehensive plans for replacing the image resizer with byte-stable output, including lanes, gates, rollback, and flaky test handling. However, RPZ more directly addresses the core requirement of byte-for-byte identical outputs by comparing old and new resizers on a golden set of images with SHA-256 hashes, whereas SSP focuses on deterministic behavior of the new library alone. RPZ also explicitly quarantines the flaky test to avoid scope creep, includes cost-control measures for CI runs, and details a dry-run of the rollback script in staging. SSP is solid but slightly less precise on the old-vs-new comparison and lacks explicit cost control. Overall, RPZ is more aligned with the request's critical success criteria.",
  "verdict": "RPZ"
}
